          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What counts as insomnia rather than just a few bad nights?",
          "answer": "Insomnia is not defined by how many hours you sleep. It is defined by a mismatch between the sleep you get and the sleep you need, and by how that affects your day. The general clinical reference (Insomnia, condition summary) says both parts must be present: trouble sleeping despite having enough opportunity to sleep, and daytime effects. Sleeping six hours and feeling fine is not insomnia.\n\nA few bad nights, often linked to a stressful event, usually settle on their own. The same reference calls this short-term insomnia.\n\nChronic insomnia disorder is the longer-term version. The sleep medicine guidance (Chronic insomnia: cognitive behavioral therapy as first-line treatment) describes it as trouble falling asleep, staying asleep, or waking too early, happening at least three nights a week for at least three months. The daytime effects can include tiredness, poor concentration, low mood, or worse performance at work or school.\n\nIf your sleep problems have lasted a while and are affecting your days, it is reasonable to raise them with a clinician. The guidance notes that other conditions, such as sleep apnea, restless legs syndrome, depression, anxiety, pain, and some medicines or substances, can look like insomnia, so a clinician may check for these.\n\nThese sources do not cover how to tell your own case apart from these patterns. That needs a clinician.\n\nSources: Chronic insomnia: cognitive behavioral therapy as first-line treatment (guideline); Insomnia (general clinical reference).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
